WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:03.140
- [Narrator] This is Nyan Cat.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:05.730
He's part cat, part Pop-Tart.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.710
The GIF of a rainbow-casting feline

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.520
was a viral meme back in 2011.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.790
Fast forward to February 2021,

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:15.210
the original GIF was
sold at an online auction

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.740
for 300 Ether,

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.060
the cryptocurrency that
powers the Etherium network.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:24.150
That was equivalent to
nearly $600,000 at the time.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:27.100
You may be asking how someone
can own the original copy

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.040
of a GIF that was pervasive
around the internet.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:34.410
It's because it was sold as
an NFT or non-fungible token,

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:38.260
which acts like a digital
certificate of authenticity.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.330
The market for NFTs ballooned in 2020,

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:44.550
climbing to a market cap
of at least 338 million

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.450
from about 41 million in 2018.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.740
But skeptics are asking
whether these assets

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.290
are really worth the
value assigned to them,

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.380
especially if all it takes to view them

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:55.863
is an internet connection.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.010
To understand how NFTs work,

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:05.480
first you need to understand
what fungibility is.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.910
Fungibility refers to
the ability of an asset

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:09.660
to be exchanged or substituted

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.400
with similar assets of the same value.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.780
A simple example of a
fungible asset is currency.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.650
Say you have five one-dollar
bills in your wallet.

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.870
You may not want to carry
around so much change

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:24.170
so you exchange them for
a single five-dollar bill.

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:26.710
The value of your money
is still five dollars

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.623
regardless of the fact it's
now in a different form.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.230
Non-fungible assets are the opposite.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:34.230
Each one is unique

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:37.970
and can't be easily substituted
for something similar.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:39.370
Think of the "Mona Lisa."

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.180
It's an original piece of art.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.880
It couldn't be swapped out for, say,

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.580
a "Mona Lisa" poster
from the Louvre gift shop

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:48.320
because the poster doesn't
hold the same value.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.250
- The idea behind NFTs is that

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:51.951
you have this digital signature

00:01:51.951 --> 00:01:53.850
in the way that a great work of art

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.885
might bear the signature of
the person who created it

00:01:55.885 --> 00:01:58.717
so you can always go and
look at the original and say,

00:01:58.717 --> 00:02:01.530
"Yes, this is the real
one. This is authentic."

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:05.010
A NFT winds up having that
through the blockchain

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.690
where the information is recorded.

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:10.180
- [Narrator] NFTs are cryptocurrencies

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.890
but unlike fungible
cryptocurrencies like bitcoin,

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:15.110
they are completely unique.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:17.430
They exist as a string
of numbers and letters

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.190
stored on a blockchain ledger.

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:22.260
This information can contain
who owns the digital asset,

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.550
who sold it, and when it was sold.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.170
This information is also encrypted,

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:29.280
ensuring the NFT's
authenticity and scarcity.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.420
In doing so, they fix a difficult problem

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.550
for digital creators on the internet:

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.140
how to make your creation scarce

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.330
and therefore more valuable.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.610
- So, with NFTs, you wind
up having the scarcity

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.130
because they are non-fungible
and because of that,

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.130
there's only one of these
tokens that can exist.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:47.990
It can't be traded for anything similar

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:50.480
because there is nothing
similar that exists.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.360
And so, you wind up getting

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.690
that scarcity and that limitation

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.280
which helps drive up some of the prices.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.700
- [Narrator] One of the early applications

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.050
of creating this scarcity

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.720
was the digital collectible
game Cryptokitties,

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.180
which emerged in 2017.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.360
Users were able to buy, trade, and breed

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.060
digital cat collectibles.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.840
Each new cat was an NFT

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:13.890
which certified its
originality and ownership.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.960
Since then, NFTs have been applied

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.420
to video games, digital
art, and sports memorabilia.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:21.420
One example is NBA Top Shot,

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.610
which allows users to procure a collection

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.300
of digital basketball highlights,

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.840
like a video clip of a posterizing dunk.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.010
All highlights are NFTs and
have become big business.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.570
By mid-March, NBA Top Shot had clocked in

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:36.350
over $338 million in sales

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.830
since it went live in October 2020.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:40.820
Non-fungible tokens are making their way

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.240
into the mainstream art world as well.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.010
Auction house Christie's opened bidding

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:48.060
for its first purely digital art NFT.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.880
Bids rocketed into the
millions of dollars.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.250
- People are really excited about NFTs

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:57.200
because, unlike with the
blockchain underlying bitcoin,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.650
you can do a lot more
complex things with it.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:02.050
You can wind up setting terms within it

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:04.085
such as, you know, the original creator

00:04:04.085 --> 00:04:08.018
upon each resale of this
asset will get x-amount.

00:04:08.018 --> 00:04:11.490
Or you can take it and you can have an NFT

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.810
that itself mints other NFTs.

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:15.820
And it winds up having all of these

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:18.750
really untested applications.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.500
The surface has only been scratched

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.520
in terms of its potential.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:24.190
- [Narrator] Along with
all the hype around NFTs,

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.410
experts have raised some concerns.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:28.810
One issue is that not all NFTs verify

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.560
the person selling a digital art piece

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.820
is actually the original creator.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.950
This is difficult particularly
in online marketplaces.

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:39.170
- One of the concerns is
that anyone can essentially

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:41.687
go on a lot of these marketplaces and say,

00:04:41.687 --> 00:04:44.730
"I am the person who created this token,"

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:47.070
and it can be really hard to verify that,

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:49.640
especially if you don't
know who they actually are

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.210
or they're saying
someone that they're not.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:53.100
- [Narrator] And some are skeptical

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.430
that ownership alone makes
digital assets valuable.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:57.950
When it comes to digital art,

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:00.370
a buyer owns the original digital painting

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:02.900
but the person can't keep
others from copying the image

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.050
and sharing or changing it online.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.657
- You have had a lot of scams

00:05:06.657 --> 00:05:09.080
in cryptocurrencies just historically

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:10.570
and so the concern is that, you know,

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:11.830
people are hyping these up.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:15.240
They're saying, like, "Go
buy these NFTs. It's great."

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.210
But in reality, you know,

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:19.950
there's always gotta be a
buyer for there to be a seller

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:22.417
and people might decide
all of a sudden, you know,

00:05:22.417 --> 00:05:23.930
"Let me cash in my profits,"

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:26.300
and someone's going to
be stuck holding the bag.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.580
- [Narrator] Proponents are bullish

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.600
on the potential for NFTs

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.740
but critics are wary that it may be

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.877
a digital bubble in the making.

00:05:33.877 --> 00:05:36.877
(suspenseful music)

